%N A401663 Irregular table read by rows: Place n circles with radius 1 with centers at the polar coordinates (1, t), 0 <= t < n: T(n,k) is the number of k-sided finite regions formed, for n >= 2, k >= 2, in the resulting graph.
%C A401663 As Pi is irrational, no two circles' centers can be diagonally opposite across the origin, thus every circle will intersect every other circle in the graph. Therefore, and similar to A401601, the total number of regions, vertices and edges follow simple quadratic formulas - see Formulas below.
%C A401663 It appears from the graphs studied that only 2, 3 and 4 sided regions are generated. This is likely true for all n since the creation of a 5-sided region would require the intersection of a 4-sided region by an arc that was not a simple rotation around the origin of any of the existing edges of that region, but that is impossible since all circles in the graph are rotations about the origin of the first circle.
%C A401663 The number of regions with 2 sides, which have one vertex at the origin, appears to always be an odd number and shows nondecreasing values as n increases. There are ranges of n where the 2-sided count stays constant, e.g., T(n,2) = 69 from n = 69 to n = 91. In the graphs studied the n values that end these constant ranges have a form n = 44*k + 4. This is likely due to the fact 44 mod (2*pi) = 0.01770..., so after every 44th circle the next circle is very close to a circle already present. When one places additional circles after this the fourth additional circle has a trailing edge that lies very close to the leading edge of the circle three back. This causes an intersection very close to the origin, creating new 2-sided regions and removing existing 3-sided regions. See the attached images for n = 44, 45, 48.
%C A401663 The number of 3-sided regions, which for n >= 5 form the bordering regions of all the graphs, increases in steps of 3 as n increases in the ranges of n that show a constant number of 2-sided regions, while outside of these ranges the count decreases by 1 as n increases. The number of 4-sided regions increases constantly but has different step size increases inside and outside of these same n ranges of constant 2-sided region count. It is not know if the above observations for the number of 2, 3 and 4 sided regions holds as n -> infinity.
